# P51 — João's restructuring suggestions (4 Oct): Chapter 2 trimmed where it repeated Chapter 1 or later
# chapters (renumbering note, the cross-border scenario, the SecuPi critique); §6.11's restated list of
# limitations replaced by a pointer to §3.7.
EDITS = [{'id': 'P51-01', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': "A further explanation of why the conflict has not been addressed is procedural. In the Commission's 2022 proposal, the six-month deletion obligation appeared in Article 46 and the Secure Processing Environment provisions in Article 50; both were renumbered on adoption in 2025 to Articles 68 and 73 respectively. A substantial part of the published commentary on EHDS secondary use was written against the proposal numbering, with the deletion obligation and the environment's security requirements analysed in separate literatures that were never subsequently brought onto the same page.", 'new': 'The renumbering of these obligations between the 2022 proposal and the adopted text, from Articles 46 and 50 to Articles 68 and 73, is one reason the commentary has treated them apart (Chapter 1, Section 1.4).', 'why': 'João: Ch2 trim; said in §1.4'}, {'id': 'P51-02', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': "The conflict is structural and operational. When a Norwegian patient is treated under MyHealth@EU in another Member State, the cross-border access event is logged in the foreign NCPeH's ATNA-compliant audit repository for the period its deployment binding sets. During that window, the patient retains the right to request erasure of personal data under Article 17. If the erasure request is granted, the audit record loses its referential integrity: it points to data that no longer exist. If the erasure request is refused on Article 17(3)(b) grounds, the controller must demonstrate that the retention is genuinely necessary for compliance with a specific legal obligation, which is the recommendation the European Data Protection Board made in February 2026 (Matheson, 2026). When the same patient's data move through the secondary-use pathway and reach a Norwegian Secure Processing Environment under a Helsedataservice permit, the Article 68(12) deletion ceiling applies. Yet the same environment must retain identifiable access logs for at least one year under Article 73(1)(e), and the audit records of the cross-border exchange that initiated the secondary-use request remain subject to the cross-border floor. Practitioner and supervisory commentary consistently reads the erasure obligation as extending to all storage locations of the personal data, including backups and logs (see, e.g., the Norwegian legal commentary summarised in Berngaard, 2024), which is precisely why the audit trail cannot be quietly carved out of the conflict.", 'new': 'In the cross-border pathway the conflict takes the form Chapter 1, Section 1.2 describes: a granted erasure request leaves an audit record pointing at data that no longer exist, and a refused one must be justified as necessary for a specific legal obligation, as the European Data Protection Board recommended in February 2026 (Matheson, 2026). Practitioner and supervisory commentary reads the erasure obligation as extending to all storage locations of the personal data, backups and logs included (see, e.g., the Norwegian legal commentary summarised in Berngaard, 2024), which is why the audit trail cannot be carved out of the conflict.', 'why': 'João: Ch2 trim; scenario in §1.2'}, {'id': 'P51-03', 'file': 'Chapter2_LitReview_v2_FINAL.md', 'old': 'The technique is not without criticism. SecuPi (2025) argues that cryptographic erasure introduces operational complexity in row-level encryption scenarios and that auditable key management adds rather than removes overhead, making it inappropriate as a general solution for the right to erasure in heavily distributed systems. Chapter 6, Section 6.4 engages it.', 'new': 'SecuPi (2025) criticises it as operationally costly in row-level encryption; Chapter 6, Section 6.4 engages that critique.', 'why': 'João: Ch2 trim'}, {'id': 'P51-04', 'file': 'Chapter6_Discussion_v1.md', 'old': 'The thesis makes one bounded claim and prices every limitation into it in advance. The proofs are informal, not machine-checked. The demonstration covers generated instances under one seed, not all instances. Key destruction is simulated in program state and says nothing about deployment assurance. The synthetic records carry US Core profiles, not Norwegian ones. The cryptographic primitives are established technique, not novel construction. The closest prior art is a patent claiming key-expiry selection for retention-purge conflicts, disclosed and distinguished (Chapter 2, Section 2.4; Chapter 4, Section 4.10). The interpretive move at the centre of the thesis is an argument without authority behind it. None of these was claimed, so none of them reduces what was: a contribution that states its boundaries can be checked at them.', 'new': 'The limitations that bound the claim are stated once, in Chapter 3, Section 3.7, and engaged where they bite. None of them was claimed, so none of them reduces what was: a contribution that states its boundaries can be checked at them.', 'why': 'João: repeated limitation statements'}]
LOST_OK = ['article 17', 'article 17(3)(b)', 'article 50', 'article 68(12)', 'article 73(1)(e)', 'hedge not been', 'number 12', 'number 17', 'number 2', 'number 2.4', 'number 2025', 'number 3', 'number 4', 'number 4.10', 'number 68', 'number 73']   # mentions now made once, in Ch1 §1.2/§1.4, §3.7 and the surviving sentences
